1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,228
Salaam. This is Liam, in for Bear, with a short film for INFO 7375, Computational

2
00:00:05,248 --> 00:00:10,127
Skepticism for AI. You might think a good skeptic doubts everything. You can't, and

3
00:00:10,147 --> 00:00:13,626
you shouldn't try. The real question is what to doubt.

4
00:00:13,696 --> 00:00:17,822
Three terms. Radical doubt: asking what would have to be true for a claim to be

5
00:00:17,842 --> 00:00:22,746
wrong. Risk: how likely a claim is to be wrong, and what it costs if it is. And a

6
00:00:22,766 --> 00:00:25,338
provisional code: rules you act by while you're still doubting.

7
00:00:25,408 --> 00:00:29,416
In his Meditations, published in sixteen forty-one, Descartes set out, once in his

8
00:00:29,436 --> 00:00:34,374
life, to tear down every belief he could find any reason to doubt, and rebuild from

9
00:00:34,394 --> 00:00:39,331
the foundations. He says it was safe to go that far because he was after knowledge,

10
00:00:39,351 --> 00:00:39,951
not action.

11
00:00:40,021 --> 00:00:43,719
This course borrows his move as one question you can ask of any claim: what would

12
00:00:43,739 --> 00:00:48,133
have to be true for this to be wrong? The answer isn't a mood. It's a list of things

13
00:00:48,153 --> 00:00:49,295
you can go and check.

14
00:00:49,365 --> 00:00:53,320
But you can't run that question on everything. Every check costs time. Doubt every

15
00:00:53,340 --> 00:00:57,295
claim you meet, and you never get to act, and the work just waits.

16
00:00:57,365 --> 00:01:00,972
Descartes saw this. In his Discourse on the Method, from sixteen thirty-seven, he

17
00:01:00,992 --> 00:01:05,437
says that while you pull down your house to rebuild it, you need another house to

18
00:01:05,457 --> 00:01:10,180
live in. So while he doubted, he kept a provisional code: a few rules to act by.

19
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:14,468
One rule: a traveler lost in a forest shouldn't wander from side to side, or stand

20
00:01:14,488 --> 00:01:18,177
still. Pick a direction and walk straight, and you'll likely end up somewhere better

21
00:01:18,197 --> 00:01:22,680
than the middle of the forest. When you can't tell what's true and you have to act,

22
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:24,004
act on what's most probable.

23
00:01:24,074 --> 00:01:28,530
So aim the doubt. Ask two things about a claim: how likely is it to be wrong, and

24
00:01:28,550 --> 00:01:32,510
what would it cost if it were? Together, that's risk. Treat this as a way of

25
00:01:32,530 --> 00:01:33,753
thinking, not a measured model.

26
00:01:33,823 --> 00:01:37,874
The chair you're sitting on has held you for years, and if it failed, you'd land on

27
00:01:37,894 --> 00:01:41,705
the floor. Don't doubt it. Sit down. Most claims in a working day live in this

28
00:01:41,725 --> 00:01:44,100
corner: act on them, and fix them if they break.

29
00:01:44,170 --> 00:01:48,750
A few claims land over here: a triage score on a patient, a medication dose, the

30
00:01:48,770 --> 00:01:53,637
number your job depends on. If one of those is wrong, someone pays for it. Those get

31
00:01:53,657 --> 00:01:54,212
the doubt.

32
00:01:54,282 --> 00:01:58,415
The rule is proportion. The more it costs to be wrong, the more doubt a claim gets.

33
00:01:58,435 --> 00:02:02,568
Cheap to be wrong: act, and watch. Costly to be wrong: stop, and ask what would have

34
00:02:02,588 --> 00:02:05,988
to be true for it to be wrong, before the world answers for you.

35
00:02:06,058 --> 00:02:09,778
Your turn. Write down five claims your work relied on this week, and paste this into

36
00:02:09,798 --> 00:02:13,517
Claude: Here are five claims my work relied on this week: paste them. For each one,

37
00:02:13,537 --> 00:02:17,724
help me say what it would cost if it were wrong, and rank them by that cost. Then,

38
00:02:17,744 --> 00:02:22,165
for the top one or two only, ask: what would have to be true for this to be wrong?

39
00:02:22,185 --> 00:02:25,671
List conditions I can check. Then check two things yourself. Are the top two really

40
00:02:25,691 --> 00:02:28,943
the ones that could hurt someone? And could you test each condition this week?

41
00:02:29,013 --> 00:02:33,420
Doubt What Your Work Depends On. At Nik Bear Brown.
